# Teaming Agreement Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — 501(c)(3) Nonprofit Research Institute
EIN: {{ ein }} | Incorporated in {{ state }}

## 1. Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") serves primarily as a Research Institution (RI) partner for small businesses on STTR proposals and other collaborative research efforts. This business model requires the Organization to enter into teaming arrangements frequently and with a variety of partners. This policy establishes the standards for evaluating, approving, executing, managing, and terminating teaming arrangements so that the Organization protects its interests, complies with federal requirements, and maintains the integrity of its research mission.

## 2. Scope

This policy applies to all teaming arrangements in which {{ org_name }} partners with one or more external organizations for the purpose of submitting research proposals, performing grants or contracts, or conducting joint research. It covers all directors, officers, employees, and agents involved in identifying, negotiating, or managing such arrangements.

## 3. Types of Teaming Arrangements

This policy governs the following categories of collaborative relationships:

| Type | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **STTR/SBIR Partnership** | The Organization serves as the RI partner to a small business concern (SBC) on a Small Business Technology Transfer or Small Business Innovation Research proposal or award. This is the Organization's primary teaming model. |
| **Joint Proposal** | The Organization co-proposes with one or more entities on a federal or non-federal research solicitation, with each party contributing a defined scope of work. |
| **Subcontractor/Subrecipient** | The Organization issues or accepts a subcontract or subaward as part of a larger research program. (Subaward issuance is also governed by the Subaward and Subrecipient Monitoring Policy.) |
| **Consortium Membership** | The Organization joins a multi-entity consortium or center to pursue a research agenda, pooled funding, or shared infrastructure. |
| **CRADA Partnership** | The Organization enters into a Cooperative Research and Development Agreement with a federal laboratory under 15 USC 3710a. |

Informal collaborations that do not involve cost sharing, joint proposals, or contractual obligations (e.g., conference co-presentations, letter-of-support exchanges) are outside the scope of this policy.

## 4. Pre-Teaming Due Diligence

Before entering into any teaming arrangement, the Executive Director (or designee) shall conduct and document the following assessments of the prospective partner:

### 4.1 Capability Assessment

- Verify that the partner possesses the technical expertise, personnel, facilities, and past performance relevant to the proposed scope of work.
- For STTR partnerships where the partner is the SBC: confirm that the firm meets SBA size standards and ownership/control requirements at the time of proposal.

### 4.2 Financial Stability

- Review available financial information (e.g., recent audited financial statements, Form 990 for nonprofits, Dun & Bradstreet report) to assess the partner's ability to sustain performance through the anticipated period of the arrangement.

### 4.3 SAM.gov and Debarment Verification

- Confirm that the partner has an active registration in SAM.gov (required for federal awards).
- Verify that the partner and its principals are not suspended, debarred, or proposed for debarment by searching the SAM.gov exclusions database. This check must be completed before execution of the teaming agreement and documented in writing. (See also the Suspension and Debarment Compliance Policy.)

### 4.4 Export Control Screening

- For any partner that is a foreign entity, has foreign ownership or control, or employs foreign nationals in key personnel roles: conduct restricted party screening per the Export Control and Technology Control Policy (Section 9) and assess whether the proposed collaboration triggers deemed-export or direct-export obligations.
- If the proposed work involves ITAR-controlled or EAR-controlled technology, coordinate with the Export Control Officer before proceeding.

### 4.5 Documentation

The results of all due diligence steps shall be recorded in writing and retained in the teaming arrangement file. A teaming agreement shall not be executed until due diligence is complete and satisfactory.

## 5. Required Teaming Agreement Provisions

Every teaming agreement shall be in writing and shall include, at a minimum, the following provisions:

1. **Scope of Work.** A clear description of the research objectives, technical approach, and deliverables that each party is responsible for performing.
2. **Roles and Responsibilities.** Designation of the lead organization (prime contractor or lead proposer), the RI partner, and any other participants, with an unambiguous allocation of tasks.
3. **Intellectual Property Allocation.** A written IP agreement specifying ownership, licensing rights, and restrictions for all foreground IP (created under the arrangement) and background IP (pre-existing IP contributed by each party). For STTR awards, the IP agreement must comply with 15 USC 638 and the SBA STTR Policy Directive, which require allocation of IP rights between the SBC and the RI before the start of work. The IP clause must address at minimum: (a) ownership of inventions and patents; (b) rights to technical data; (c) rights to software and source code; and (d) the government's rights under Bayh-Dole (for federally funded work). See also the Intellectual Property and Invention Disclosure Policy.
4. **Cost Sharing and Funding.** The budget allocation, payment terms, invoicing procedures, and any cost-sharing or matching-fund commitments by each party.
5. **Data Rights.** Rights of each party to use, disclose, and publish data generated under the arrangement, including any restrictions required by the sponsor.
6. **Publication Rights.** Each party's right to publish research results, subject to pre-publication review periods (typically 30-60 days) for patent evaluation and sponsor compliance.
7. **Conflict Resolution.** A dispute resolution mechanism (e.g., escalation to senior leadership, followed by mediation, then binding arbitration) and choice of governing law.
8. **Term and Termination.** The effective date, duration, conditions under which either party may terminate (for convenience and for cause), and wind-down obligations upon termination (including disposition of data, equipment, and incomplete deliverables).
9. **Confidentiality.** Mutual obligations for protecting proprietary and confidential information exchanged during the teaming arrangement.
10. **Compliance Flow-Down.** A clause requiring both parties to comply with all applicable federal regulations, including but not limited to 2 CFR Part 200, FAR/DFARS (where applicable), export control laws, and the terms of any resulting award.

## 6. Approval Authority

| Anticipated Total Value of Arrangement | Approval Required |
|----------------------------------------|-------------------|
| Up to $1,000,000 | Executive Director |
| Above $1,000,000 | Board of Directors |
| Any value, if involving export-controlled work (ITAR/EAR) | Board of Directors |

"Anticipated total value" means the combined value of work to be performed by all parties over the full period of performance, including options. When the value is uncertain at the time of teaming (e.g., a proposal has not yet been awarded), the approval shall be based on the proposed budget.

The Executive Director may execute teaming agreements within their approval authority without prior Board action, but shall report all new teaming arrangements to the Board at the next regular meeting. For arrangements requiring Board approval, the Executive Director shall present the due diligence findings and a summary of material terms for Board review before execution.

## 7. Standard Teaming Agreement Template

The Organization shall maintain a standard teaming agreement template that incorporates the provisions required by Section 5. The template shall be reviewed by legal counsel and updated at least annually. The Executive Director may authorize deviations from the template for specific arrangements when warranted, provided the deviations are documented and the required provisions of Section 5 are preserved.

For STTR partnerships, the template shall include the SBA-required IP allocation agreement as an exhibit or appendix.

## 8. Exclusivity and Non-Compete

### 8.1 When Exclusivity Is Appropriate

Exclusivity clauses (restricting either party from teaming with competitors on the same solicitation) may be included when:

- The solicitation permits only one proposal per team and the parties have made substantial joint investment in proposal development;
- A party is contributing unique or proprietary capabilities that would be competitively compromised if shared across competing teams; or
- The sponsor requires exclusivity as a condition of participation.

### 8.2 Limits

Exclusivity provisions shall be narrowly scoped:

- **Subject matter.** Limited to the specific solicitation, topic, or program — not to an entire research domain.
- **Duration.** Limited to the proposal and award period. If no award results, exclusivity expires no later than **six (6) months** after proposal submission or the agency's announced decision date, whichever is earlier.
- **Geography.** Limited to the jurisdiction of the specific solicitation, unless broader scope is justified in writing.

The Executive Director shall not agree to exclusivity terms that materially restrict the Organization's ability to pursue its research mission with other partners.

## 9. Conflict of Interest

### 9.1 Disclosure Obligation

Before entering into a teaming arrangement, all {{ org_name }} personnel involved in negotiating or approving the arrangement must disclose any existing or potential conflict of interest with the prospective partner, including:

- Financial interests (ownership, employment, consulting fees, or other compensation);
- Family relationships with principals or key personnel of the partner;
- Prior or concurrent teaming, employment, or board service with the partner; and
- Any other relationship that could reasonably be perceived as creating a conflict.

### 9.2 Management

Disclosed conflicts shall be evaluated and managed in accordance with the Organization's Conflict of Interest Policy. An individual with a material conflict shall be recused from the approval decision for that teaming arrangement.

## 10. Record Retention

All teaming arrangement records — including the executed agreement, due diligence documentation, amendments, correspondence, performance records, IP agreements, and termination notices — shall be retained for the **duration of the teaming arrangement plus five (5) years** from the date of expiration or termination. If the teaming arrangement is associated with a federal award, the longer of this retention period or the period required by 2 CFR 200.334 (three years from final expenditure report) shall apply.

Records shall be stored and managed in accordance with the Organization's Document Retention and Destruction Policy.

## 11. Annual Review of Active Teaming Arrangements

The Executive Director shall conduct an annual review of all active teaming arrangements and present a summary to the Board of Directors. The review shall assess:

1. Whether each arrangement remains aligned with the Organization's mission and strategic priorities;
2. Whether performance obligations are being met by both parties;
3. Whether the partner's SAM.gov registration and debarment status remain current;
4. Whether any IP, publication, or data-rights issues have arisen;
5. Whether any arrangement should be renegotiated, renewed, or terminated; and
6. Whether the standard teaming agreement template requires updates based on lessons learned.

The date and findings of each annual review shall be recorded in the board meeting minutes.

